  body: "# Income and capital distinction in JSA(IB) and Income Support\n\n## Authored interpretation\n\
    \nThe captured guidance says income is not defined in the cited law and offers payment-series and
    payment-period characteristics to distinguish it from capital, with an exception to the usual treatment
    of capital instalments.\n\n## Scope and exceptions\n\nThese are source-specific classification questions,
    not a universal accounting definition. Repeated payments are not automatically income: the capital-instalment
    note and special provisions must be considered. JSA(Cont) has a different scope for other income.
    No equivalence with Pension Credit or UC is asserted.\n\n## Inspected source passages\n\n- [DMG 28002,
    PDF page 3](https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/69a16975f534e7e99adaea96/dmg-ch-28.pdf#page=3)
    — DMG Vol 5 Ch 28: Income other than earnings.\n- [DMG 28000, PDF page 2](https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/69a16975f534e7e99adaea96/dmg-ch-28.pdf#page=2)
    — DMG Vol 5 Ch 28: Income other than earnings.\n\n## Evidence and review status\n\nModel-assisted
    interpretation of selected passages in the 15 September 2026 frozen source snapshot. Specialist review
    is required. Source acquisition, source revision, legal effective dates and this record’s authoring
    time are separate.\n\nThis is an independent experimental publication, not an official DWP document,
    individual advice, an entitlement decision or an executable rule. Machine-extracted text may alter
    reading order, footnotes and characters. No PDF visual inspection, whole-chapter coverage or behavioural
    evaluation pass is claimed by this record.\n\n- `dmg-vol5-ch28`: PDF SHA-256 `bcaea58ee40a9dca60a21b0f79c525a1f15e95c6b8315d6aaa73f0f53dd5460f`;
    extracted pages SHA-256 `f7e554c1af4c7d8b915524f441c1680c5bea7429ac8fff9fd18bc0c925f25c31`.\n"
  semantic_relations:
  - target: term/jsais-gross-other-income
    predicate: http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#related
    rationale: Having identified other income, the chapter separately describes gross-income
      treatment and its permitted disregards.
    evidence:
    - page: page/dmg-vol5-ch28/0003
      quote: "28002 Income is not defined in law1. It can normally be separated from capital because a
        payment of\nincome\n\n\n1. forms part of a series of payments, whether or not they are made regularly
        or\n\n\n2. is made for a period of time or\n\n\n3. satisfies both the conditions in 1. and 2..\n\
        \n\nNote: Where capital2 is being paid by instalments each payment will be capital unless DMG
        28530 et seq\napplies.\n\n\n                1 JS Act 95; SS CB Act 92; 2 Lillystone v. Supplementary
        Benefits Commission (1982) FLR;\n                                Morrell v. Secretary of State
        for Work and Pensions [2003] EWCA Civ 526"
      locator: DMG 28002
    - page: page/dmg-vol5-ch28/0003
      quote: "28003 [See DMG Memo IS/JSA 64] The DM should take into account the gross amount of any income
        1.\nReferences in this Chapter to income means income other than earnings. When calculating gross
        income\ndisregard the sums in DMG 28008 - 28010 and DMG 28150 et seq. No disregards should be
        made for\nany other expenses or deductions.\n\n\n                                            \
        \               1 JSA Regs, reg 103(1); IS (Gen) Regs, reg 40(1)"
      locator: DMG 28003
